[{:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-25T03:39:32.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-25T03:39:32.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rails", :item {:feed-id "sean-goedecke", :item-id "https://seangoedecke.com/dont-anthropomorphize-big-tech-companies/", :title "Don't anthropomorphize big tech companies", :link "https://seangoedecke.com/dont-anthropomorphize-big-tech-companies/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-25T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "Bryan Cantrill has a great line from a talk where he cautions against anthropomorphizing1 Oracle CEO Larry Ellison. I think it’s also a mistake to treat any tech company like a person. Here’s the full quote: You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don’t anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just mows the lawn, you stick your hand in there and it’ll chop it off, the end. You don’t think ‘oh, the lawnmower hates me’ — lawnmower doesn’t give a shit about you, lawnmower can’t hate you. Don’t anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don’t fall into that trap about Oracle. Companies are machines, not persons Here are some examples of anthropomorphizing companies: My company really owes me for that hard thing I did I’m fighting the good fight against my company on issue X (e.g. accessibility) and they hate me for it My company is vindictively retaliating against me because of that mistake I made My company and I are partners in the mission to make the world a better place All of these are mistakes. Your relationship to the tech company that employs you is not like a relationship with a person. It’s more like your relationship with your car2. If you drive poorly or fail to change your oil, your car may stop working or even kill you, but it doesn’t love or hate you. It has no sense of fairness. It’s simply a dangerous, useful machine: useful enough to be worth getting into despite the risks. Anthropomorphizing it makes it harder to avoid those risks3. Why is it so hard to avoid this trap? First, humans anthropomorphize everything by default. Second, whenever you’re interacting with the company, you’re interacting with a person: your manager, your colleague, someone in HR, and so on. Third, when you’re in the power of some entity, it’s psychologically comforting to think of it like another human. This is the same principle that caused early humans to attribute lightning to the power of Zeus, because Zeus (unlike electrical imbalances in storm clouds) can be placated with prayer and sacrifice. Disproportionate efforts and rewards Relationships with machines are very different from relationships with people. The biggest difference is that machines do not respect effort. If you put a lot of work into doing a favor for a friend, your friend will appreciate it even if the favor itself is not a big deal. Conversely, if you help somebody out at trivial cost to yourself, that help will be relatively unappreciated. But a car doesn’t care how much effort you put into maintaining it. If you spend hours replacing a car’s engine, but the brake line is damaged, the car won’t remain functional out of respect for your effort on the engine: instead, the brakes will fail, killing you. Tech companies work like this. Everyone who’s worked at one for long enough knows that it’s very hard to predict what work will be high-impact. The project you slaved over for months will often get a “nice, now what’s next” reaction, while the tool you built in fifteen minutes (or the question you answered in a Slack channel) will sometimes get wild praise and recognition. This can drive engineers a little mad, because they’re anthropomorphizing their tech company, and a person who behaved like this would be dangerously unstable. But if you treat your company like a machine, this shouldn’t be surprising at all. It’s hard to predict high-impact work, but it’s not impossible. The best way to operate at a big tech company — as I’ve said again and again — is to try and develop this skill, and to spend most of your time looking for the low-effort high-reward wins. It’s hard to do that if you’re secretly imagining your company as a person. Anthropomorphism is a dangerous shorthand A natural objection here is to point out that tech companies (unlike cars) are composed of people who might indeed owe you, hate you, love you, and so on. According to this view, when people say “my company hates me”, they aren’t making the mistake of anthropomorphizing a corporation, they’re using shorthand to say “my boss and skip-level hate me”. I think this is still a dangerously sloppy way to think. In my experience, engineers who think they’re hated are often not. Managers in large tech companies do hold grudges, but (particularly the higher up you get) are tolerably cold-blooded. You don’t get to senior director or VP by needlessly throwing away useful tools. I can recall a few cases4 where engineers told me they were hated, but I later found out5 that managers considered them dangerous or unreliable: loose cannons whose usefulness didn’t outweigh their tendency to go off the rails and cause damage. From the perspective of the engineer being forced out of the company, it feels like you’re being run out of town by people who hate you. But if you want to avoid being run out of town, it’s useful to recognize when you’re being treated as a defective tool. It’s also useful to keep in mind the difference between “my manager hates me” and “my company hates me”, because managers are often temporary. You can sometimes just wait out a bad relationship with your manager. You’ll have a slightly uphill battle with the next one — since the handoff won’t be positive — but it’s certainly possible to get back on track. If you can figure out why your manager hates you, you might also be able to fix it (or at least mitigate6 it). Don’t go too far in the other direction Engineers who’ve learned not to anthropomorphize tech companies sometimes go too far in the other direction, by treating companies as a computer program. This is the wrong kind of machine: tech companies aren’t people, but they aren’t programs either. They operate on incentives and illegible interpersonal relationships, not on consistent logical steps. The classic example of this is the engineer who reads the company handbook cover-to-cover and makes sure they follow all the rules. When they’re looking to be promoted, they take the role guidelines and make sure they’re following them point-by-point. They take what they’re told very literally: when the company says “X is our top priority”, they internalize that. The problem with this is that handbooks, guidelines and internal communications are the legible superstructure of a massive illegible web of dependencies that make up the actual operations of any company. That superstructure will not cover the real criteria for promotions, or the rules that are designed to be broken. The “top priority” of the superstructure can rapidly shift, leaving literal-minded engineers confused and disheartened. Conclusion It’s understandable to feel ill-treated by a company, or to harbor resentment — particularly since there are actual humans at the company who are involved in that treatment of you. But companies themselves don’t hold grudges, even as they force out people who they’re incentivized to force out7. It can even be a mistake to anthropomorphize very senior managers, whose work personas are more identified with the machine the higher up you go8. I suppose at the top you have Larry Ellison, as Bryan Cantrill says, who is better modeled as a lawnmower than as a human being. No relationship with an individual person at your company will save you from being careless with the machine: it will chew you up and spit you out. You need to learn how to drive the car. This is scary, because it means that sometimes you will just get punished for no reason. But it’s also good news, because it means that you can get disproportionate rewards for relatively little effort, so long as you make the right decisions. Treating as a human. ↩ I like this example better than Cantrill’s lawnmower because cars are more central to our lives, like jobs are, and are also far more dangerous. ↩ For instance, if you’re prone to thinking “my car loves me, it wouldn’t spin out when I take this hard turn on gravel”, you are more likely to crash. ↩ Though certainly not all. Grudges do exist. ↩ Of course, this is my interpretation of the situation, and could be wrong. ↩ There are a range of techniques here, ranging from “do all your paperwork defensively” to “find some out-of-the-way project” to “ingratiate yourself with your skip-level”. ↩ For instance, people who make their managers look bad, or who publicly point out the inevitable inaccuracies in legible reports, or who campaign against the current big push the company is making. ↩ I say “work personas” because these people often have a rich personal life that is largely firewalled off from their work selves. For more on how managers come to identify with the rules of the organization, I recommend Robert Jackall’s Moral Mazes, a book that has informed much of my thinking and that I will some day write a long-form review of. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "... didn’t outweigh their tendency to go off the rails and cause damage. From the perspective of the...", :matched-terms ["rails"], :source :content}]}]